  "articleBody": "Thinking about a low-threshold shower is a smart move, especially if you're looking for improved accessibility or just a sleek, modern look. But before you dive in, there are a few things you’ll want to consider to ensure it’s the perfect fit for your home. First, can it actually replace your existing bath or shower space? Most of the time, yes, but it’s good to confirm the exact measurements and layout possibilities. You’ll also want to think about the accessibility needs. Are you just looking for ease of entry, or do you need space for a shower chair or even a wheelchair? This impacts the size and design of the shower base and overall footprint. Drainage is another critical point. A low-threshold shower relies on precise sloping for proper water flow, so you'll want to make sure the drain location works with your chosen base. Lastly, decide if you want a prefabricated unit, which can be quicker to install, or a custom tiled build for a more personalized look. Either way, ensuring the subfloor is sound and the waterproofing is top-notch is vital. When in doubt, a professional can really help navigate these choices and ensure a smooth, worry-free installation. We’re always happy to offer guidance in the greater Denver area!",
